 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N_{pixels} \cdot N_{steps})$. The first pass is $O(N_{vertices})$ (constant for a box). The second pass dominates, scaling linearly with the number of pixels and marching steps.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N_{data} + N_{pixels})$. Requires storage for the volume data texture, the transfer function texture, and the intermediate render target.
-*   **Performance:** The use of a fixed number of steps (default 50) provides consistent performance but may result in aliasing or blockiness if steps are too low. Increasing steps improves quality but lowers FPS [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Simple implementation on GPU; decouples geometry intersection from optical calculation; efficient use of hardware blending.
-*   **Limitations:** Fixed step size may be inefficient for sparse volumes (marches through empty space); no early ray termination in the basic description; requires the volume to be contained within a simple bounding box.
-*   **When to use:** Real-time visualization of dense volumetric data where approximate rendering is acceptable and hardware support for float render targets is available.
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot S)$, where $N$ is the number of pixels (screen resolution) and $S$ is the number of marching steps per ray [S2, unofficial]. The first pass is $O(N)$ for rendering the bounding box faces.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(V + N)$, where $V$ is the volume data size (stored in a texture) and $N$ is the screen resolution (for the intermediate render target) [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** The method is efficient on GPUs due to parallel ray processing [S1, unofficial]. The number of steps directly impacts FPS; 50 steps is a common default [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Decouples boundary intersection from integration, allowing for flexible shading and MIP modes [S1, unofficial]. Supports interactive features like cutting planes [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Fixed number of steps may lead to aliasing or slow convergence for complex volumes [S2, unofficial]. Requires sufficient texture memory for large volumes [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The method is implemented in the `INTMeshVolumeRendering` class, part of the WebGL Volume Rendering library by Interactive Network Technologies. It was authored by Vincent Dumestre and Nathan Krupa. The code is hosted on GitHub [S1, unofficial]. It is designed for use with the Three.js library.
+This method is implemented in the `INTMeshVolumeRendering` class for WebGL-based volume visualization in Three.js [S2, unofficial]. It was developed as part of a visualization course project at TU Wien, focusing on interactive 3D volume rendering using WebGL and d3.js [S1, unofficial]. The technique builds on the general principles of two-pass rendering, specifically separating bounds determination from ray marching [S1, S2, unofficial].
 
